# Pi4U: installation and example runs

This guide describes how to build Pi4U with the separately maintained [Torc-Lite runtime](https://github.com/CEID-HPCLAB/torc_lite). Install Torc-Lite first, then build the Pi4U engines you need.

The commands below assume a Linux/POSIX environment and a Bash-compatible shell. They use a user-owned installation prefix and require no administrator privileges once the prerequisites are available.

## 1. Prerequisites

Install the following packages through your system package manager, or load the corresponding modules on your HPC system:

| Dependency | Used for |
| --- | --- |
| Git and GNU Make | Retrieving and building the code |
| C and C++ compilers | Pi4U engines and Torc-Lite |
| Fortran compiler | TORC Fortran support and PNDL |
| MPI implementation with C, C++, and Fortran wrappers | TORC-based execution; examples use `mpicc`, `mpic++`, and `mpif90` |
| GNU Scientific Library (GSL), including development headers | TMCMC, ABC-SubSim, DRAM, subset simulation, and differentiation tools |
| Autoconf, Automake, and associated build tools | Regenerating Autotools files when needed; required for the PNDL source checkout described below |
| gnuplot (optional) | Sample visualization |

Use the same MPI implementation and compatible compilers when building Torc-Lite, Pi4U, and PNDL. For standards-compliant Torc-Lite execution, MPI must provide `MPI_THREAD_SERIALIZED` or `MPI_THREAD_MULTIPLE`; see the [Torc-Lite documentation](https://github.com/CEID-HPCLAB/torc_lite) for details.

## 2. Install Torc-Lite

Choose a parent directory for the two source checkouts, then run:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/CEID-HPCLAB/torc_lite.git
cd torc_lite

export TORC_PREFIX="$HOME/usr/torc"
./configure CC=mpicc F77=mpif90 --prefix="$TORC_PREFIX"
make
make install

export PATH="$TORC_PREFIX/bin:$PATH"
```

`TORC_PREFIX` is an example installation location; choose another user-writable directory if preferred. If `configure` or generated build files are missing, run `autoreconf -fi` before configuring. Wrapper names vary by MPI installation; substitute the appropriate names on your system.

Check that the installed helper scripts are available:

```bash
command -v torc_cflags
command -v torc_libs
torc_cflags
torc_libs
```

Pi4U Makefiles use these scripts to obtain the TORC include and linker flags. Restore the `PATH` setting in every new shell or batch job that builds Pi4U.

Build and run a TORC demo from the Torc-Lite root directory:

```bash
make -C demo
export TORC_WORKERS=2
mpirun -np 2 ./demo/masterslave
```

Here, `-np 2` selects two MPI processes and `TORC_WORKERS=2` selects two TORC workers per process. TORC also uses communication threads. Allocate sufficient CPU resources, and use your site's MPI launcher and process-binding settings on a cluster.

If the loader cannot find a TORC shared library after installation, add the directory containing that library to the runtime library search path. For the example prefix, this is typically:

```bash
export LD_LIBRARY_PATH="$TORC_PREFIX/lib${LD_LIBRARY_PATH:+:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH}"
```

Check the installation layout; some systems use `lib64`. This setting is unnecessary when TORC is linked statically.

## 3. Retrieve Pi4U

Return to the parent directory containing the Torc-Lite checkout:

```bash
cd ..
git clone https://github.com/CEID-HPCLAB/pi4u.git
cd pi4u
export PI4U_ROOT="$PWD"
```

Pi4U and Torc-Lite are separate repositories. Pi4U's TORC-based builds use the installed runtime through `torc_cflags` and `torc_libs`.

## 4. Build and run TMCMC

```bash
cd "$PI4U_ROOT/inference/TMCMC"
make use_torc=1 use_omp=0

export TORC_WORKERS=2
mpirun -np 2 ./engine_tmcmc
```

Run the executable from its engine directory so that its configuration files are found. The supplied model in `fitfun.c` is a two-dimensional Rosenbrock example. Edit `tmcmc.par` for algorithm settings and `fitfun.c` to integrate another model.

The run produces generation files such as `curgen_db_000.txt`, `curgen_db_001.txt`, and subsequent generations.

TMCMC also supports the following builds:

| Execution mode | Build command | Run command |
| --- | --- | --- |
| TORC/MPI | `make use_torc=1 use_omp=0` | `mpirun -np 2 ./engine_tmcmc` |
| OpenMP | `make use_torc=0 use_omp=1` | `OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 ./engine_tmcmc` |
| Serial | `make use_torc=0 use_omp=0` | `./engine_tmcmc` |

Run `make clean` before switching execution modes or changing compiler flags, because existing targets may otherwise be reused. In the inherited TMCMC Makefile, plain `make` builds the serial version.

## 5. Other inference and optimization engines

Build each engine from its own directory. The following commands refer to the current source layout; execution options are specific to each Makefile.

| Component | Directory relative to the Pi4U root | Build command |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CMA-ES | `singleopt/cmaes` | `make use_torc=1` |
| AMaLGaM | `singleopt/AMaLGaM` | `make` |
| NSGA-II | `multiobj/nsga2` | `make use_torc=1 use_omp=0` |
| ABC-SubSim | `inference/ABC_SubSim` | `make use_torc=1` |
| DRAM, sequential | `inference/DRAM` | `make` |
| Subset simulation | `rare/subset` | `make` |

AMaLGaM's default target builds both serial and TORC executables. Subset simulation uses TORC directly, without a `use_torc` switch. The DRAM executables run sequentially.

### CMA-ES

```bash
cd "$PI4U_ROOT/singleopt/cmaes"
make use_torc=1
mpirun -np 2 ./engine_cmaes
```

Model evaluations are defined in `fitfun.c`. Consult `cmaes_initials.par` and the other CMA-ES configuration files for settings. The TORC build also produces the experimental asynchronous executable `engine_cmaes_async`.

### Subset simulation

```bash
cd "$PI4U_ROOT/rare/subset"
make
mpirun -np 2 ./engine_ss1
```

`engine_ss1` uses predefined levels. To run the adaptive-level version instead:

```bash
mpirun -np 2 ./engine_ss2
```

Settings are provided in `subset.par`. Output includes sample files such as `samples_000.txt`. The two examples may write to the same filenames; preserve outputs before running the next example.

### ABC-SubSim

```bash
cd "$PI4U_ROOT/inference/ABC_SubSim"
make use_torc=1
```

The resulting executable is `abc_subsim_torc.exe`. It requires command-line parameters, including population size and stopping criteria. See [`README-ABC.txt`](inference/ABC_SubSim/README-ABC.txt) for the argument list and instructions for editing `fitfun.cpp` and `problem.h`.

## 6. Numerical differentiation with PNDL

PNDL requires TORC and MPI Fortran support. The inherited build contains paths to a bundled `torc_lite` directory. The commands below override those paths for the library build and avoid building the legacy PNDL test programs, whose Makefiles also contain bundled-runtime paths.

```bash
cd "$PI4U_ROOT/derivatives/pndl"
autoreconf -fi
./configure CC=mpicc F77=mpif90 --prefix="$HOME/usr/pndl"

make -C src AM_FFLAGS="$(torc_cflags) -I." \
            AM_CFLAGS="$(torc_cflags) -I."
```

Use `F77` here: PNDL's configure script detects a Fortran 77 compiler through `AC_PROG_F77`. An MPI wrapper such as `mpif90` can compile the fixed-form Fortran sources.

Build the differentiation tools:

```bash
cd "$PI4U_ROOT/derivatives/tools"
make MPIF90=mpif90
```

Their Makefile links directly to `../pndl/src/libpndl.a`, so installing PNDL is not required for these tools. Run them from `derivatives/tools`, where `grad.par` supplies their configuration:

```bash
mpirun -np 2 ./fd_grad
mpirun -np 2 ./fd_deriv
mpirun -np 2 ./sa_deriv
```

- `fd_grad`: differentiation-step selection using gradients and Romberg extrapolation.
- `fd_deriv`: gradient and Hessian calculation through PNDL.
- `sa_deriv`: experimental stochastic approximation of the Hessian.

For a permanent build-system update, replace the bundled TORC paths in PNDL's `src/Makefile.am` and `test/Makefile.am` with installed-runtime flags, then regenerate the Autotools files. Building the PNDL tests additionally requires updating their TORC include and linker flags.

## 7. Visualization

Install gnuplot, then build the display tools:

```bash
cd "$PI4U_ROOT/display"
make
```

The generation viewer accepts:

```text
./display_gen <datafile-prefix> <last-generation> <dimension> [var1] [var2]
```

For example, if a two-dimensional TMCMC run produced generations 000 through 004:

```bash
./display_gen ../inference/TMCMC/curgen_db 4 2 1 2
```

Set the last-generation argument to a generation that actually exists. Generation filenames follow `<prefix>_<generation>.txt`, with zero-padded generation numbers. The parameter columns are followed by a model-evaluation column.

## 8. Coupling external models

The [`coupling`](coupling/) directory contains examples for calling external programs from a model-evaluation function. These include a Rosenbrock example and a [simple external Python model](coupling/simple_model/README.md).

Follow each example's instructions to build its driver, prepare the model files, and configure parameter exchange. External models can use their own programming language and execution environment. Python and any model-specific dependencies are required only for examples that use them.

## 9. Troubleshooting

| Problem | What to check |
| --- | --- |
| `torc_cflags` or `torc_libs` not found | Install Torc-Lite and add its installation `bin` directory to `PATH`. |
| `torc.h` or `torcf.h` not found | Check the include path printed by `torc_cflags` and the installed headers. For PNDL, use the overrides in Section 6. |
| GSL headers or libraries not found | Install the GSL development package or load its module; add the correct include and linker paths to the engine Makefile. |
| `mpicc`, `mpic++`, or `mpif90` not found | Load the MPI environment or substitute your installation's wrapper names. |
| TORC library not found at runtime | Check the installed library directory and runtime library search path. |
| Configuration files not found | Run from the engine/example directory containing its configuration files. |
| Changing build options has no effect | Run `make clean` before rebuilding. |
| Autotools files missing or outdated | Run `autoreconf -fi` in the relevant library directory; inspect any reported compatibility errors. |

Older Makefiles may require compiler-specific adjustments on modern systems. For nonstandard MPI wrappers, override the variables actually used by the engine, such as `CC`, `CPP`, `LD`, or `MPIF90`, or edit its Makefile. For GSL, preserve the existing flags when adding installation paths.

For support, open an [issue](https://github.com/CEID-HPCLAB/pi4u/issues) with the component, compiler and MPI versions, exact build/run command, relevant configuration, and error output.
